Definition : Measures how hard code is to understand (not just test). Unlike cyclomatic complexity, cognitive complexity: Penalizes nesting (nested if is worse than flat if) Ignores shorthand structures (x && y doesn't add complexity) Rewards language features that reduce cognitive load Example : // Cyclomatic: 4, Cognitive: 1 (shorthand logical operators)
if x && y && z && w { do_something();
} // Cyclomatic: 4, Cognitive: 7 (nesting adds cognitive load)
if x { // +1 if y { // +2 (nested) if z { // +3 (deeply nested) if w { // +4 (very deeply nested) do_something(); } } }
} Why it matters : Cognitive complexity predicts how long it takes to understand code. High cognitive complexity means: New developers struggle Bugs hide in complex logic Refactoring is risky How to reduce : Flatten nesting (use early returns) Extract complex conditions into named functions Use guard clauses Leverage pattern matching

